I agree with the majority. The CFAA defines “exceeds authorized access” as using authorized access to obtain information “that the accesser is not entitled so to obtain.” The word “so” is best read as a term of reference pointing to the manner already stated in the statute: obtaining information through a computer one is authorized to access. It does not incorporate every external purpose-based limit found in an employer policy, contract, or state law.

Van Buren was authorized to enter the database and to retrieve license-plate records. His motive was improper, and other laws or department discipline could address that misconduct. But his purpose did not make the information one he was “not entitled so to obtain” under the CFAA’s text. The dissent’s reading would make federal criminality turn on violations of ordinary computer-use rules, risking prosecution for personal email, news reading, or minor terms-of-service violations. It also fits poorly with the statute’s structure, which treats “without authorization” and “exceeds authorized access” as complementary access-based categories, and with the civil provisions’ focus on technological harm. Congress could have enacted a purpose-based computer-misuse law; this statute does not clearly do so.

