I would join the Court’s judgment. Section 404(a) of the First Step Act makes eligibility turn on whether the defendant previously received a sentence for a “covered offense,” defined as a violation of a federal criminal statute “the statutory penalties for which were modified” by the Fair Sentencing Act. Terry’s offense was possession with intent to distribute an unspecified amount of a Schedule I or II controlled substance under §841(b)(1)(C). That subsection carried the same penalty range before and after 2010. The Fair Sentencing Act changed the quantity thresholds in §§841(b)(1)(A) and (B), thereby modifying the penalties for offenses involving at least 50 grams or 5 grams of crack, but it did not amend subparagraph (C). It is not enough that the overall penalty scheme changed or that prosecutors’ charging options shifted; the statute asks whether the penalties for the offense of conviction were modified. They were not.

Justice Sotomayor’s concurrence comes closest to my view because she accepts the textual result while candidly explaining the residual unfairness. The outcome is bad for the country because it leaves people like Terry—sentenced for a small amount of crack under the old 100-to-1 regime and career-offender guidelines—without a chance to seek resentencing, even though Congress later rejected that disparity’s severity. But the proper remedy is legislative, not judicial.

