The majority’s reading is correct. Section 1396k(a)(1)(A) requires assignment of “any rights … to payment for medical care from any third party.” That language naturally includes a tortfeasor’s obligation to pay future medical expenses. Ahlborn’s anti-lien exception turned on the medical/nonmedical distinction, not on whether Medicaid had already paid the precise expenses. Where Congress wanted to confine recovery to services already paid, it did so expressly, as in §1396a(a)(25)(H). Reading that narrower automatic-acquisition provision as limiting the broader assignment provision would erase Congress’s chosen language and make the assignment provision redundant in part. The anti-lien provision remains important, but it is overcome here by the express assignment exception. The “lifetime assignment” concern is manageable because the statute is best read to cover rights possessed while the person is a Medicaid beneficiary and related to the covered injury.

Separately, I think the practical outcome is bad. Allowing a state to satisfy past Medicaid expenditures from funds allocated to future medical care risks stripping a catastrophically injured beneficiary of resources needed for ongoing care. Even if textually permitted, that result sits uneasily with Medicaid’s protective purposes and may create harsh incentives in settlement allocation. Congress, not the Court, would need to correct it if it wants a different balance.

