I would vote with the majority on the legal question. Section 2401(a) does not say challenges must be brought within six years of promulgation; it starts the clock when “the right of action first accrues.” In 1948, and under the Court’s ordinary limitations precedent, that meant when the particular plaintiff has a complete and present cause of action. Under APA §§702 and 704, a plaintiff cannot seek review until she is injured by final agency action. Therefore, a later-injured plaintiff such as Corner Post does not accrue until it suffers that injury. Congress repeatedly enacted special review statutes tied to entry or promulgation when it wanted repose; §2401(a) is not such a statute. The dissent’s policy worries are real, but they cannot convert a general accrual statute into a finality deadline.

Separately, the practical outcome is troubling. Allowing each newly injured or newly formed entity to bring a facial attack on an old rule weakens reliance interests and invites strategic relitigation. The better policy would be an APA-specific repose period. But the Court properly leaves that choice to Congress.

