I would have sided with the dissent. ACCA’s text defines a state “serious drug offense” by reference to a “controlled substance” as defined in the CSA. That definition is not a frozen list; the CSA expressly contemplates annual schedule updates. When a criminal statute incorporates another provision, the usual presumption is to apply the referenced law in effect when the federal offense occurs, absent clear retroactive intent. McNeill is distinguishable: it addressed the state maximum sentence for the prior conviction, not the federal definition of the substance. ACCA is backward-looking about the fact of conviction, but Congress chose a federal benchmark that can change. If Congress wanted past schedule status to control, it could have said “a substance that was controlled at the time of the prior offense.” Instead, it used the present-tense cross-reference. The rule of lenity also supports the narrower reading because ACCA imposes a severe mandatory minimum.

Policy-wise, the majority’s outcome is bad. It preserves a fifteen-year mandatory minimum based on technical schedule mismatches and outdated substances, increasing sentencing severity and disparity without a clear congressional command. Courts should not expand harsh mandatory minimums by implication.

