Legally, I would side with the dissent. AEDPA asks not whether the Mississippi Supreme Court’s waiver ruling was wrong, but whether it was unreasonable. Mississippi may require preservation of specific Batson rebuttal arguments, see Ford v. Georgia, and the state court’s reading of the record was at least reasonable. Counsel’s contemporaneous objections were statistical; after the judge said the objection was preserved, counsel repeated only the racial-composition statistics; the comparative-pretext argument appeared later in a single sentence; and trial counsel later conceded she had not preserved pretext. Pitchford’s reading is plausible, but §2254(d) requires more than plausibility; the record must compel rejection of the state court’s finding. Therefore, I could not say the state court’s waiver decision was beyond fairminded disagreement.

Separately, the outcome is good. Four of five Black jurors were struck in a capital case, and the trial court never completed Batson’s third step. Remanding for meaningful scrutiny promotes equal protection and public confidence, even though I think the majority’s AEDPA analysis is too aggressive.

